WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.276
Press A and the cat jumps. Press
B and the very same cat spins.

2
00:00:09.496 --> 00:00:13.304
One sprite, two inputs, two
scripts, two different outputs.

3
00:00:13.804 --> 00:00:19.098
A program can wait for an input: a
key, a button, a click on a sprite.

4
00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:24.009
Press A. That input has a script
of its own, and the cat jumps.

5
00:00:24.229 --> 00:00:27.944
Press B. A different script
runs, and the same cat spins.

6
00:00:28.164 --> 00:00:32.390
Click the cat and it says hello.
Press the space bar and it hides.

7
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.883
Four inputs, four scripts, four
different outputs, from one sprite.

8
00:00:37.383 --> 00:00:43.698
Before you build an object, plan it.
What is its input? What is its output?

9
00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:47.634
The cat's input is the A
key. Its output is a jump.

10
00:00:47.854 --> 00:00:50.640
The door's input is the
player walking into it.

11
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.157
Its output is the door
opening and playing a creak.

12
00:00:54.377 --> 00:00:58.789
The score sign waits for the ball
to hit the goal, then adds one.

13
00:00:59.009 --> 00:01:02.260
Nothing happens without
an input, so plan both.

14
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.379
This program is in three named parts:
the reset, the jumps, the greeting.

15
00:01:08.599 --> 00:01:12.361
Run part one on its own. The cat
goes home. That part is fine.

16
00:01:12.581 --> 00:01:17.225
Run part two. The cat spins,
and we wanted two jumps.

17
00:01:17.445 --> 00:01:21.021
So the bug is in part two, and
nowhere else in the program.

18
00:01:21.241 --> 00:01:26.349
Testing one part at a time tells you
where, instead of leaving you to guess.

19
00:01:26.849 --> 00:01:31.493
Fix the block in the part that
failed. Take out spin, put in jump.

20
00:01:31.713 --> 00:01:35.567
Now run part two again. Two
jumps. That part passes.

21
00:01:35.787 --> 00:01:39.410
Only when every part passes
does the whole program pass.

22
00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.438
The next program had two bugs,
hiding in two different parts.

23
00:01:43.658 --> 00:01:47.419
Part by part found them one at
a time, which is far easier.

24
00:01:47.919 --> 00:01:52.331
There is a bad way to find a bug:
changing things until it works.

25
00:01:52.551 --> 00:01:55.895
That sometimes works, and it
teaches you nothing at all.

26
00:01:56.115 --> 00:02:00.898
The good way is debugging systematically.
Split the program into parts.

27
00:02:01.118 --> 00:02:05.019
Run one part. Check its result
against what you expected.

28
00:02:05.239 --> 00:02:09.744
Move on only when that part is
right. The error has nowhere to hide.

29
00:02:10.244 --> 00:02:15.631
Each input runs its own script, so a
different input gives a different output.

30
00:02:15.851 --> 00:02:20.495
Plan an object by naming its input
and its output before you build.

31
00:02:20.715 --> 00:02:25.591
Test one part at a time. The
part that fails holds the bug.

32
00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:30.176
Fix that block, run the part again,
and the error has nowhere to hide.
